    correlacionAnalistasTitulo: "Correlation of an index with analyst recommendations",
    correlacionAnalistasDesc: "Checks whether TODAY's analyst consensus (from Yahoo Finance) is related to the actual return each index component had afterwards, over 1, 2, 3 and 6 months. It makes no sense to look back further than 6 months: recommendations change fairly often, so one from over half a year ago no longer says much about the current consensus. Since Yahoo's consensus runs from 1 (strong buy) to 5 (strong sell) — the opposite of what's intuitive —, the score used here is 5 minus that value, so a positive correlation means what you'd expect: better consensus, better subsequent performance. Some follow-the-herd effect from investors reacting to recommendations is to be expected, especially over shorter periods.",
    correlacionAnalistasBotonCargando: "Calculating correlation...",
    correlacionAnalistasBoton: "Calculate correlation with analysts",
    correlacionAnalistasResumenTitulo: "Pearson correlation by period",
    correlacionAnalistasConclusionTitulo: "Conclusion",
    correlacionAnalistasDetalleTitulo: "Detail by stock",
    col1Mes: "1 month",
    col2Meses: "2 months",
    col3Meses: "3 months",
    col6Meses: "6 months",

    correlacionAnalistasIndicesTitulo: "Correlation of the indices with analyst recommendations",
    correlacionAnalistasIndicesDesc: "Same as the tool above, but for several indices at once: one row with the 4 Pearson correlations (1, 2, 3 and 6 months) for each index checked below, to compare whether the analyst-consensus effect is stronger in some than in others. Analyst consensus can only be checked one stock at a time, so the more indices you check — and the more components they have —, the longer it will take.",
    correlacionAnalistasIndicesEtiquetaSeleccion: "Indices to include:",
